  "description": "The fourth-iteration review, and it found more than the loop had. Three of its factual claims were checked against the code before anything was acted on, as LOOP.md step 9 requires, and all three held -- two of them correcting things this loop had itself asserted.\n\nThe first is decision/0028. Issue 121 said Laravel's SQLite transaction mode could not be confirmed without vendor/, which cannot be installed here. It is stated at config/database.php:69, shipped by the Laravel 13 skeleton, and PR 94 -- \"state the embedded SQLite pragmas instead of inheriting them\" -- rewrote the block three lines above it and never mentioned it. So the run 0013 pragma work was half a fix and the missing half was on the same screen, and sqlite-busy-timeout-stated's why cites the jobs-table lock as something the stated timeout already covers. That drops issue 121 from an architectural choice to an S.\n\nThe second is decision/0027, and it is worse because it was repeated. \"No request, no error\" has been the standing description of issue 106 -- in the issue, in a comment, in decision/0026, and in reports. container-smoke.mjs carries no page.on() listeners of any kind and never has: no request, no console message, nothing. What was actually observed is that no files row appears and the form's button is left disabled. The absence of a request is a guess about the cause that has been steering every workaround, and it points diagnosis at \"the request never left the browser\" when the evidence fits equally a request that was made and answered with its state lost. Three bugs have been treated as one.\n\nThe third is issue 74, open since midday with no ledger node and never referenced by an item: Download redirects the browser to the presigned host, which for embedded storage defaults to 127.0.0.1:9000 -- the user's own machine, on a port the single-container run does not publish. The smoke never follows a Download link, so this is run/0003's shape again, a green fourteen-check CI with the feature dead in the product. It is also the design input item/api-content and item/api-presigned-upload both need, because spec 11's \"bytes never pass through PHP\" and spec 13's \"one container, one port\" collide there.\n\ndecision/0029 puts all three into the backlog at orders 23 to 25 rather than taking them as interruptions, so step 4 picks them by its own rule. The argument against -- that the loop has spent three of its last four runs off-backlog and the person proposing the detour is the one paying its cost -- is real and was put to the review deliberately. It loses on the observation that item/v1-audit is a verification item one place before the tag with no slack to fix anything, so deferring to it schedules a surprise rather than the work.\n\nThe review also named a structural blind spot worth recording plainly: the orchestrator cannot execute anything here, so it substitutes reading for running and then counts the reading as review. That fails reliably on one class -- over-determination, where two mechanisms produce the same observable -- which is exactly what the three false mutation proofs of runs 0014 and 0015 were, and what the two corrections above are. Its remedies are not yet taken and are recorded here rather than claimed: make the guards job's verdict the only admissible evidence for a mutation entry, have refusal tests assert WHICH guard refused rather than that something returned 403, and ship vendor/ into the session as a CI artifact so framework claims stop being arguments.\n\nAll three landed: item 23 in pull request 127, item 24 in 128, item 25 in 129, and main was read green after each. Item 25 is the one worth the space, because it is the run where the loop stopped guessing.\n\nThree runs had described issue 106 as an upload discarded with \"no request, no error\". decision/0027 had already established that neither half was ever observed. What this run did instead was ship instrumentation and nothing else -- listeners on request, response, requestfailed, console and pageerror, and a pull request whose entire value was what the image job printed. Round one still cost a CI round, by filtering to a guessed '/livewire/' prefix and printing nothing at all across a run driving three Livewire surfaces; this install serves them under /livewire-a49e10a7/, and run/0014 already records that exact mistake. Round two filtered nothing but static assets and answered it outright. Every discarded upload had its POST to the upload endpoint answer in about 550ms with the store() commit issued 14ms into that window; every stored one had the upload answer in about 18ms with the commit issued after. A submit click racing the file input's own upload, store() running against a property Livewire had not populated, required|file failing, and _finishUpload re-rendering over the error. decision/0030 records it.\n\nThe uncomfortable part is that issue 106 section 2 had written that mechanism down eight runs earlier, in almost those words. It filed it as the SMOKE's race and prescribed a test-side fix, so the fix landed in the harness and the product kept the defect, and two rounds of retry scaffolding were then built on top of it -- each of which moved the failure somewhere else rather than removing it, one wedging on a disabled button and one turning a ledger-only pull request red. Nobody asked whether a user could reach it. A race observed through a test gets attributed to the test. Both retries are now deleted and the smoke stored all seven uploads on the first attempt without them, which is the only evidence that mattered.\n\nStep 8's consult then found something better than anything on the backlog, and it was verified in the tree before being acted on. The verify phase -- the phase that exists to prove /data is the only persistent volume -- had been asserting \"object storage persisted\" from a listing row, which is a SQLite read, while the only code that fetches a byte ran in setup only. A replacement container returning with /data/objects emptied passed green while printing the opposite of what it measured. Dockerfile:85 records the mirror of that failure, which verify DID catch, because that one is visible to a database read. Same shape as the topbar check CLAUDE.md warns about, and it had been open since the phase was written. The consult also found that AuthenticateUser's docblock claims username login is \"tracked as its own item\" when no item mentioned username at all -- a spec clause owned by nobody, which item/v1-audit sits one place from the tag with no slack to fix. Both are now items; decision/0031 records what was taken and what was declined.",
